course: biology
lesson: 262
title: The human body deeper: lungs, stomach and guts
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Under your lungs lies a dome of muscle called the diaphragm; when it pulls down, air rushes into your lungs, and when it relaxes, air flows back out, about 20,000 times a day without you thinking about it.
explain: Inside the lungs the air pipes branch like an upside-down tree and end in hundreds of millions of tiny air sacs, where oxygen slips into the blood through very thin walls and carbon dioxide slips out.
explain: Food takes a long journey: the stomach churns it in strong acid, then the small intestine, about 6 metres of tube folded up in your belly, soaks it into the blood through millions of tiny finger-like bumps.
explain: That rumble before lunch is your stomach and guts squeezing gas and liquid along in waves, and they keep squeezing even when they are empty.
explain: In cartoons, Tom swallows something and you can see its shape sliding down inside him, and gum is said to stay in your stomach for seven years; in real life your gut keeps everything moving, and swallowed gum comes out in a few days.
fact: The diaphragm, a dome of muscle under the lungs, pulls down to suck air in, and hiccups are sudden jumps of the diaphragm.
fact: An adult's lungs hold several litres of air, but a normal breath is only about half a litre, and you breathe about 20,000 times a day.
fact: The lungs have about 500 million tiny air sacs called alveoli, and spread out flat their surface would cover roughly half a tennis court; so many tiny sacs give far more surface for oxygen to pass through than one big bag could.
fact: Oxygen passes into the blood through the thin walls of the air sacs, and carbon dioxide passes out to be breathed away.
fact: Breath is warm and carries water vapour, which turns into tiny droplets you can see as a cloud in cold air.
fact: Tobacco smoke coats the lungs with tar and damages the tiny hairs that clean them, causing cancer, heart disease and breathing problems; smokers' lungs turn dark grey, while healthy lungs are pink.
fact: A tummy rumble, called borborygmi, is the stomach and guts squeezing gas and liquid along in waves.
fact: Swallowed chewing gum is not digested but passes through in a few days; swallowing lots at once can block the gut, so gum belongs in a bin.
fact: Stomach acid is very strong and kills germs and breaks down food, and a thick layer of slime stops the stomach from digesting itself; the lining replaces itself every few days.
fact: The small intestine of an adult is about 6 metres long, though books give different lengths, and tiny finger-like bumps called villi soak food into the blood; the long, bumpy tube gives a huge surface for soaking up food.
fact: A meal takes from about one to three days to travel all the way through the gut.
fact: In 1822 Alexis St. Martin was shot, and his wound healed leaving a hole into his stomach; the doctor William Beaumont put food on strings through it to watch digestion; today tests on people have careful rules, and patients must be kept safe and must freely agree.
fact: Fizzy drinks hold carbon dioxide gas, which bubbles out in the warm stomach and escapes upwards as a burp; in space, burping is tricky because gas and food do not separate without gravity.
fact: The liver, weighing about 1.5 kilograms, is the biggest organ inside the body, does over 500 jobs such as cleaning the blood and storing energy, and can grow back from a part left behind.
exercise: Which muscle pulls down to suck air into your lungs? | The diaphragm | The biceps | The heart | The stomach
exercise: What are hiccups? | Sudden jumps of the diaphragm | Bubbles bursting inside the heart | The stomach sneezing all by itself | Air stuck deep inside the ears
exercise: About how much air do you breathe in during a normal breath? | About half a litre | About ten litres | About a teaspoon | About a bucketful
exercise: About how many times a day do you breathe? | About 20,000 times | About 200 times | About 2 million times | About 20 times
exercise: What are the tiny air sacs in the lungs called? | Alveoli | Villi | Valves | Cilia
exercise: About how many air sacs are in a pair of adult lungs? | About 500 million | About 500 | About 5 | About 5 thousand
exercise: Spread out flat, about how big would the inside surface of the lungs be? | Roughly half a tennis court | About the size of a stamp | About the size of a pillow | The size of a whole country
exercise: What happens in the tiny air sacs? | Oxygen goes into the blood and carbon dioxide comes out | Food is turned into blood | Air is turned into water | Germs are made into vitamins, which are then sent to the stomach
exercise: Why can you see your breath on a cold day? | Water vapour in warm breath turns into tiny droplets | Cold air turns into smoke as it goes in | Your lungs make little clouds in winter to keep you warm | Breath freezes into snow
exercise: What does tobacco smoke do to the lungs? | Coats them with tar and damages their cleaning hairs | Makes them stronger and pinker, like exercise does | Cleans out all the dust and germs inside them | Nothing, only the throat is harmed
exercise: What makes a tummy rumble? | The stomach and guts squeezing gas and liquid along | The stomach growing little teeth when it is hungry | The heart beating loudly down inside the stomach | Bones rubbing in the belly
exercise: What really happens to swallowed chewing gum? | It passes through the gut in a few days | It stays in the stomach for seven whole years | It turns into a balloon inside your tummy | It sticks to the heart and stays there for good
exercise: Why doesn't your stomach digest itself? | A thick layer of slime protects its wall | The acid is far too weak to harm anything at all | The stomach is made of hard bone on the inside | The stomach empties out the acid every hour
exercise: About how long is the small intestine of an adult? | About 6 metres | About 6 centimetres | About 60 metres | About 6 kilometres
exercise: What do the villi in the small intestine do? | Soak up food into the blood | Push the air down into the lungs | Make the stomach acid for digestion | Chew the food again into tiny bits
exercise: What did William Beaumont do through the hole in Alexis St. Martin's stomach? | Put food on strings in to watch digestion | Fed him through a long straw only, for weeks | Listened to his heart | Closed it up straight away
exercise: Why do you burp after a fizzy drink? | Carbon dioxide bubbles out in the stomach and escapes up | The drink is far too cold for the stomach, so it sends it back | Sugar turns into air in the mouth | The stomach is trying to sneeze
exercise: Which is the biggest organ inside the body? | The liver | The heart | The brain | The kidney
test: In a cartoon, Tom swallows a bowling ball and you can see it roll down inside his neck, and he is fine at once. What really happens when you swallow food? | Muscles squeeze it along a long tube in waves, from stomach to guts | It falls straight down like a ball in a pipe | It sits in the throat until you jump up and down a few times to shake it down | It goes into the lungs first
test: «swallowed my gum, mum says it'll be in me 7 years lmao» What is right? | No, it passes through in a few days, though gum still belongs in a bin | Yes, it sticks to the stomach wall for seven whole years before it comes off | Yes, and then it melts | No, it goes straight into the blood
test: «book says stomach acid can melt metal, so why ain't my belly melting» What is the answer? | A thick layer of slime protects the stomach, and the lining renews every few days | The book is wrong, because stomach acid is really just as weak as plain water | The stomach is lined with metal | Acid only works when you sleep
test: Count them: a balloon needs 3 normal breaths of half a litre each. How much air is that? | One and a half litres | Three litres | Half a litre | Six litres
test: What would happen if the lungs had just one big air bag instead of millions of tiny sacs? | There would be far less surface for oxygen to pass into the blood | Breathing would be much easier, with more room for air in the chest | You would be able to hold your breath for hours | Nothing would change
test: «my uncle smokes and he's fine, so it's no big deal» What is right? | Smoking damages the lungs and heart over time and causes cancer | Smoking only harms people who are already ill with something else | Smoking cleans dust out of the lungs | It is only harmful for children
test: Why would burping be harder for an astronaut in space? | Without gravity, gas and food in the stomach do not separate | Space has no air for them to burp into | Fizzy drinks freeze solid in space as soon as they are opened | Astronauts have no stomachs in space
test: Count them: 2 friends eat lunch at noon. Each meal takes at least 1 day to go all the way through. When is the earliest it could be finished? | Around noon the next day | Around 1 o'clock the same day | Around teatime the same day | Exactly one week later, at noon
test: Why is the small intestine so long and bumpy inside? | To give a huge surface for soaking up food | To store food for the winter | To keep the stomach warm | Because it grew far too fast in childhood and got tangled
test: The ancient Greeks told of Prometheus, whose liver was eaten each day and grew back. What is true about the real liver? | It can grow back from a part left behind | It never grows back at all once it is cut | It grows back only in birds, never in people | It is replaced by the stomach after a while
test: A doctor today wanted to copy Beaumont's food-on-a-string tests on a patient. Why would it not be allowed? | Patients must be safe and freely agree, with careful rules for experiments | Food on strings is now forbidden in shops | Stomachs work differently today from how they did in the past, so his results are useless | There are no more stomachs left to study
exam: Identify the principal function of the alveoli of the lungs. | The exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide between the air and the blood | The production of sound in speech | The digestion of food prior to its absorption into the blood through the walls of the lungs | The storage of water for the body
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the fate of chewing gum upon its being swallowed. | It passes through the digestive tract within a few days, being itself undigested | It remains within the stomach for a period of seven years | It is wholly absorbed into the blood | It adheres permanently to the wall of the intestine
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the protection of the stomach against its own acid? | A thick layer of mucus covers its wall, and its lining is renewed every few days | The acid is too weak to affect any tissue | The stomach is lined with bone | The acid is discharged from the body every hour, before it can cause harm to the stomach
exam: Ascertain which organ is the largest within the human body and is capable of regeneration from a remaining portion. | The liver | The heart | The brain | The lungs
